{
	default_hide_dependency_lines: false
	default_quest_shape: ""
	filename: "warfactory_compute"
	group: "539A285D299C0F64"
	icon: "wfcore:mainframe"
	id: "1863BADEC9C99CD7"
	order_index: 2
	quest_links: [ ]
	quests: [
		{
			description: [
				"The &bComputation Mainframe&r is Warfactory's supercomputer. It supplies &bcomputation&r - measured in &bCWU/t&r (Compute Work Units per tick) - to machines that need to think: the &6Research Unit&r and the &6Radar&r."
				""
				"It is &edemand-driven&r: it only produces as much CWU as much as is being requested, and only then draws power and makes heat."
				""
				"Three kinds of parts decide what it can do:"
				"&bCPUs&r make the compute."
				"&bRAM&r caps how much compute can flow (the throughput ceiling)."
				"&bCoolers&r fight the heat the CPUs make."
				""
				"&7Provided CWU = min( total CPU compute , total RAM throughput ), minus any heat penalty.&r"
				""
				"Deliver it with a &fComputation Data Transmission Hatch&r on the mainframe and a &fReception Hatch&r on the consumer, wired together with &6copper network cable&r."
			]
			icon: "wfcore:mainframe"
			id: "1064385592420A72"
			shape: "octagon"
			size: 1.5d
			subtitle: "Start of computation"
			tasks: [{
				id: "70674227B124B833"
				item: "wfcore:mainframe"
				type: "item"
			}]
			title: "The Computation Mainframe"
			x: 0.0d
			y: 0.0d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["1064385592420A72"]
			description: [
				"&cmaxPower&r = power fed in"
				""
				"CPUs turn &epower into compute&r. At full load each CPU makes:"
				"&b  CWU = maxPower x (efficiency - 0.2)&r"
				"and draws &emaxPower EU/t&r. Higher efficiency = more compute per EU and &aless heat&r."
				""
				"Every tier has two picks - an efficient &aStandard&r and a hotter, thirstier &cHigh-output&r - so you are never locked to one chip. Older CPUs keep working, just weaker."
				""
				"Look at the &etooltip&r below circuits, some will have extra &bmainframe&r statistics, which tell you the value of the &cefficiency stat&r in the aforementioned equation."
				""
				"Slot one CPU into each &fMainframe CPU Socket&r."
			]
			icon: "wfcore:cpu_slot"
			id: "50DE46B419C8CC91"
			subtitle: "CWU = maxPower x (efficiency - 0.2)"
			tasks: [{
				id: "7781C6BC6BB39B7B"
				item: "wfcore:cpu_slot"
				type: "item"
			}]
			title: "CPUs — the compute"
			x: -1.5d
			y: 1.5d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["1064385592420A72"]
			description: [
				"RAM does not make compute - it &bcaps&r it. A mainframe can never provide more CWU/t than its total RAM throughput, no matter how many CPUs you cram in."
				""
				"Each &fMainframe RAM Socket&r holds &e4 sticks&r. A full socket is that tier's ceiling:"
				"&2• MV RAM&r   24 each  ->  full socket &b96&r"
				"&2• HV RAM&r   32 each  ->  full socket &b128&r"
				"&2• EV RAM&r   48 each  ->  full socket &b192&r"
				"&2• IV RAM&r  128 each  ->  full socket &b512&r"
				""
				"Want more than one socket's worth? Add a second RAM socket - but that costs a part slot, more CPUs and more cooling. That escalating cost is the by-design nudge to &7build another mainframe&r instead of megasizing one."
			]
			icon: "wfcore:ram_slot"
			id: "3E40F0CE6F768144"
			subtitle: "RAM throughput is the per-mainframe ceiling"
			tasks: [{
				id: "0E550094BCA5B474"
				item: "wfcore:ram_slot"
				type: "item"
			}]
			title: "RAM — the ceiling"
			x: 0.0d
			y: 1.5d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["1064385592420A72"]
			description: [
				"CPUs turn wasted power into &6heat&r. Let the mainframe get too hot and it &cthrottles&r, then &4detonates&r:"
				"&e• 90 C&r   performance sag begins - you lose CWU"
				"&c• 105 C&r  explosion"
				""
				"&9Passive:&r a &fMainframe Copper Heatsink&r vents heat to the air - stronger the hotter you run, but weak on its own. Bolt a &bCooling Fan Cover&r (LV–EV) onto its exposed face to multiply it up to &b5x&r (EV cover)."
				""
				"Through &2EV&r a comfortable build sits well under 90 C on heatsinks alone. &cAt IV&r the CPUs run so hot that passive fans stop being enough — see Liquid Cooling."
			]
			icon: "wfcore:copper_heatsink"
			id: "0E86B95017BC9B4D"
			subtitle: "sag at 90 C, boom at 105 C"
			tasks: [{
				id: "212DE98169260ACD"
				item: "wfcore:copper_heatsink"
				type: "item"
			}]
			title: "Mainframe Copper Heatsink"
			x: 1.5d
			y: 1.5d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["0E86B95017BC9B4D"]
			description: [
				"When passive fans are not enough, the &fMainframe Liquid Cooler&r pumps coolant (up to &e100 mB/t&r each) for far stronger active cooling."
				""
				"Colder coolant carries more heat:"
				"&fWater&r < &bOxygen&r < &bHelium&r < &bLiquid Nitrogen&r."
				""
				"Its cooling is &bflat&r - it does not weaken as the frame cools - so one cooler on cold coolant will hold almost any single mainframe under the sag point."
				""
				"&cAt IV this stops being optional"
				"&aHigh-Tier CPUs&r require &4tremendous&r amounts of cooling, which is where &9liquid cooling&r comes in. &3Liquid nitrogen&r is recommended at this stage. At &5EV&r and before this is a luxury."
				""
				"&7Needs a coolant supply via a fluid input hatch.&r"
			]
			icon: "wfcore:cooling_liquid"
			id: "11D5579559CD4022"
			subtitle: "Stronger cooling"
			tasks: [{
				id: "0655335A5DAF1D91"
				item: "wfcore:cooling_liquid"
				type: "item"
			}]
			title: "Mainframe Liquid Cooler"
			x: 1.5d
			y: 3.0d
		}
		{
			description: [
				"The &6Research Unit&r is the mainframe's main customer. It runs research projects from &fmaterials + power + computation&r, writing finished research to a data stick."
				""
				"Wire its &fComputation Data Reception Hatch&r to the mainframe's &fTransmission Hatch&r with optical pipes. A project lists a &bCWU/t&r demand; meet it and research runs at full speed, starve it and it crawls."
				""
				"&7Rule of thumb - a stage's research wants about half that tier's voltage in CWU/t: MV ~64, HV ~256, EV ~1024, IV ~4096.&r"
			]
			icon: "wfcore:research_unit"
			id: "2A3E44DDC97E2DBF"
			subtitle: "New recipes..."
			tasks: [{
				id: "5AACF3BB62DA01D8"
				item: "wfcore:research_unit"
				type: "item"
			}]
			title: "The Research Unit"
			x: -1.5d
			y: 3.0d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["2A3E44DDC97E2DBF"]
			description: [
				"One mainframe rarely feeds a whole base. The &bMV Network Switch&r bridges optical computation networks: it &fpools&r every attached Reception Hatch and re-serves the combined CWU/t out of every Transmission Hatch."
				""
				"Use it to:"
				"&b&rbring several mainframes into one big compute pool, or"
				"&b&rfan a single mainframe's output out to many machines."
				""
				"&7Draws a little EU per connected computation hatch. Mainframes cannot bridge on their own - the switch is what lets them share.&r"
			]
			icon: "wfcore:mv_network_switch"
			id: "5AC0238D1A9F09D5"
			subtitle: "Pool many mainframes into one compute grid"
			tasks: [{
				id: "782D180309270B0A"
				item: "wfcore:mv_network_switch"
				type: "item"
			}]
			title: "The Computation Network"
			x: -1.5d
			y: 4.5d
		}
	]
	title: "Warfactory Compute"
}
